; BookTrail.inix -- how the BookTrail database opens, and how each table is heard.
;
; BookTrail keeps the books you have read, are reading, and mean to read. It is
; built for a reader who follows authors and series, and who reads by ear or by
; finger as often as by eye: a narrator field, formats such as BARD audio and
; Bookshare text, and a catalog number for finding a book again.
;
; ONE TABLE, ON PURPOSE. Author and series are fields of the book, not tables of
; their own. That is what lets the list be ordered by author, then series, then
; volume -- the order a series reader wants -- and it is what a spreadsheet of
; books already looks like, so importing one needs no reshaping. Consistency,
; which separate tables would enforce, comes from pick lists instead: F4 on
; series, format, genre or status offers the values already in use, so a series
; is chosen rather than retyped. A series spelled two ways is the commonest cause
; of a book sorting in the wrong place.
;
; ONE ROW PER COPY (11 October 2026). A book read on Audible and again from BARD is two rows, because format,
; source, catalog number and narrator belong to a copy, not to the work: the Audible copy has its narrator and
; ASIN, the BARD copy its reader and NLS number. A row is known by author, title and source, and ordering by
; author, series and volume keeps a work's copies together. work holds the same key for every copy of one work.
;
; WHAT bookFido FILLS. bookFido gathers a reader's Audible, Kindle, Goodreads, Bookshare and BARD libraries, and
; when DbDo is installed for that user it writes them here: publisher, first published, language, length, pages,
; ISBN, progress, average rating and its count, the Wikipedia article, and the companion files it saved. What a
; reader wrote -- status, rating, reason, notes -- is filled only where it is blank, and tags only gain lines.
; Each author it found joins the author pick list, with a biography and a link.
;
; VOLUME IS TEXT, as typed. A novella between books 6 and 7 is 6.5, and some
; readers write 06. DbDo sorts text that holds only a number as a number, so
; 6.5 falls between 6 and 7 and 10 comes after 9.

[Database]

InitialTable = books

[Table:books]

; The row says who wrote it first, because a reader of series looks books up by
; author, and first-letter navigation searches the first column. Then the title,
; then where it sits in its series. Four fields, the ceiling.
SelectFields = author, title, series, volume
OrderFields = author, series, volume, title
WhereFilter =

[Table:lookups]

SelectFields = fld, val
OrderFields = fld, ordinal
WhereFilter =

[Table:maps]

SelectFields = kind, prime1, prime2
OrderFields = kind, prime1
WhereFilter =
